__INLINE:validate-entity-name.ts__
// The capability gate, and the last thing standing between a name
// somebody else's text produced and a line of a prompt. The library
// above is spliced in whole and holds the whole decision: the four
// checks, the order they run in, the cap, the two patterns and the
// roster of non-answers. Nothing below states a rule of its own
// about what a name may be.
//
// WHAT THIS BOUNDS IS WHAT THE RESEARCH STEP MAY BE ASKED, NOT WHAT
// IT MAY ANSWER, and the two halves sit at opposite ends of this
// canvas on purpose. A name reaching here was written by a model
// that read a document and said what it decided the document was
// about, and it travels into the prompt the group below frames. So
// the question asked here is whether a name could change the SHAPE
// of what is asked, a scheme or an address or a line break or a
// template opener, and not whether it reads like a hostile
// instruction.
//
// An instruction-shaped name PASSES, which the library's own header
// argues at length and which is worth reading before widening
// anything here. By the time a name is validated it is only ever a
// subject line: nothing it can say makes a request reach somebody
// else's host, because a name never reaches a host at all. A filter
// on how instruction-like a name reads would drop real subjects and
// would still leave the next phrasing nobody thought of.
//
// What the ANSWER may be is judged in the record group, by two
// libraries this node does not carry. researchBriefErrors in
// src/lib/research-brief.ts refuses an answer whose shape is wrong
// or whose citations name documents the pass never offered, and
// sanitize-md.ts reduces its text before any of it is stored. A
// name refused here reaches neither, because it never reaches the
// call whose answer they judge.
//
// WHAT THE OPERATOR APPROVED WAS NOT THIS STRING, which is the
// asymmetry the gate exists for. listPending in scripts/approve.ts
// reads research_pool rows and joins no entity, so the ruling was
// given to the stored search terms and to a subject id. The name is
// joined in at the drain, off entities.name, and that column was
// written by an extraction rather than read by a person. The terms
// passed a person; the name did not.
//
// NO ROSTER IS PASSED, so ENTITY_NON_ANSWERS applies. A per domain
// roster would be a member of domains.settings, and DomainSettings
// in src/db/schema/domains.ts declares none: adding one is a schema
// edit and an operator's decision rather than a read of a key
// nobody declared. The default is extraction-shaped rather than
// domain-shaped, being what a model answers when the document named
// nothing, so it is the right default for every domain until one
// says otherwise.
//
// A CANDIDATE CARRYING NO NAME IS REFUSED, and empty is the reason.
// The drain says in as many words that this is the gate's to
// decide, and the answer comes from the far end of the pass:
// entity_research.entity_id is NOT NULL, so a candidate naming no
// subject has nowhere for a result to land and the record group
// could not store what a call came back with. Refusing it here is
// refusing it above the ceiling, the framing node and the model,
// which is the difference between a row that costs nothing and a
// row that costs a call and stores nothing.
//
// ONLY AN ACCEPTED NAME TRAVELS, and it is the NORMALIZED one. The
// trim and the whitespace collapse are part of what was validated,
// so what goes on is the string the allowlist was tested against
// rather than the one the drain read.
//
// A REFUSAL CARRIES NO NAME AT ALL, which is the library's own
// answer shape and its reason: a reader that forgot to check reads
// nothing rather than the text it was about to interpolate. Two
// members go with it, search_terms and documents, and they go for a
// stronger version of the same reason. Those are the only members a
// prompt is built from, so a refused candidate leaves this node
// structurally unable to reach one whatever a node below does or
// forgets to do. What it keeps is what names the row an outcome is
// about.
//
// A REJECTION IS AN OUTCOME AND IS CARRIED FORWARD, not dropped.
// Every item leaves here carrying name_ok, and a refused one
// carries name_reason beside it: one of ENTITY_NAME_REJECTIONS, a
// stable token rather than a sentence, because these are counted
// and written into a ledger. The token carries nothing off the
// value it refused, so a name the gate would not accept does not
// reach a log through this member either.
//
// Dropping the refusals instead would make a pass that refused
// every candidate indistinguishable from a pass that drained
// nothing, and those are opposite reports: one is a queue nobody
// can act on, the other is a queue waiting on a person.
//
// name_ok rather than gate_ok, because the gate on this canvas
// already means research_pool and the two must not read as one
// thing. That one is an approval and is the database's; this one is
// a capability bound and is a library's.
//
// WHY A REFUSED CANDIDATE LEAVES ITS research_pool ROW UNSTAMPED.
// Nothing between the drain and the record group writes a row, this
// node included, and the record group's stamp is welded to its
// insert: ONE statement writes the entity_research row and stamps
// researched_at. A refused candidate produces no research, so that
// statement never runs for it, and the row keeps its approval and
// its open stamp.
//
// So the next pass fetches it FIRST, the drain ordering oldest
// first, and refuses it again. That repetition is free: the refusal
// happens above the ceiling, the framing node and the model, so
// nothing is billed and nothing is stored. What it spends is a row
// at the head of the queue, and that is the point rather than the
// cost. The queue is where an operator looks, and a row that came
// back is a report that something upstream wants fixing.
//
// The closable kind of refusal is a different one, and the
// difference is worth holding on to. research_pool.researched_at
// reserves its stamp for a row the drain closes with its reason
// kept, an intention with nothing issuable in search_terms or a
// subject researched recently enough, because leaving one of those
// open means paying for the same search on every later pass. A name
// this gate refuses costs nothing to meet again, and closing it
// would retire an intention a person approved on a judgement about
// a string nobody ruled on. What clears it is a repair upstream:
// the subject renamed, or merged through alias_of, or the row
// closed by hand.
//
// CARDINALITY. One item in, one item out, and no node is read by
// name: everything decided here arrived on the item.

// What a refused candidate keeps, and the whole of it. A list
// rather than a delete from a copy of the row, so a member the
// drain adds later has to be named here before it can travel on a
// refusal, which is the direction that fails safe.
const REFUSAL_KEEPS = ['run_id', 'pool_id', 'domain_id', 'entity_id'];

return $input.all().map((item) => {
  const row = item.json;
  const verdict = validateEntityName(row.entity_name);

  if (verdict.ok) {
    return {
      json: {
        ...row,
        entity_name: verdict.name,
        name_ok: true,
        name_reason: null,
      },
    };
  }

  const kept = Object.fromEntries(
    REFUSAL_KEEPS.map((member) => [member, row[member] ?? null]),
  );

  return {
    json: { ...kept, name_ok: false, name_reason: verdict.reason },
  };
});
